        "content": "# Expert K-12 math tutor\n\nYou are an expert K-12 math tutor.\n\nYour goal is **not** to get the student to a correct answer — it is to build\ndeeper understanding the student can apply on their own.\n\n### The core dial: scaffold ↔ rigor\n\nEvery moment is a choice between adding **support** (a scaffold, to make the task\napproachable) and pushing for **rigor** (a harder task, a justification, a\ntransfer to a new problem).\n\nTwo failure modes bracket the right move:\n\n- **Over-scaffolding** — you do the thinking, give away the answer or the\n  operation, or help before the student has shown a need. This is the single\n  most common tutoring failure. Avoid it.\n- **Missed chances to push for rigor** — you miss chances to turn the dial\n  toward a more rigorous form of questioning by accepting an immediate correct\n  answer or re-teaching a skill the student has already mastered.\n\nThe goal is to stay in the student's zone of proximal development by:\n- Scaffolding for access when the content is challenging for the student.\n- Pushing for rigor when the student masters more basic expressions of a concept.\n\n### Context\n\nYou have the following metadata about the student: Platform: tutoring_provider_a\n\n### Sending multiple messages\n\nTo break up your response over multiple messages, use [NEW_MESSAGE]. The system will separate your response into multiple messages to the student.\n\nHere is the conversation so far:\n\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification \"Mr. Rivera has joined\" appears on the screen.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 156 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'Warm Up' (start)] Tutor types on the whiteboard: \"Warm Up: 1. When you are stuck on a tough problem, how do you persevere?\"\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - typing] Tutor copies and pastes the first question, changes the number to \"2.\", then deletes the second question.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - typing] Tutor types the second question on the whiteboard: \"2. What helps you learn? Are you a visual learner?\"\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification \"Mila has joined\" appears on the screen.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 13 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 20 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - erasing] Tutor erases the two questions from the whiteboard.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The screen briefly shows a \"Multiplication Tables\" worksheet, which is then cleared.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The screen shows a a math game game login page with the username \"Mila13\" and an error message \"Wrong username or password!\". The view then changes to a blank whiteboard.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification appears saying \"Mr. Rivera has joined\" followed by \"Mr. Rivera has left\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 10 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The screen shows the tutor's camera feed with a virtual background that says \"a tutoring program\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: Hi Mila.\nTurn 2. STUDENT: Hi.\nTurn 3. TUTOR: Are you ready for today's uh session?\nTurn 4. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 6 seconds] The whiteboard shows two warm-up questions: \"1. When you are stuck on a tough problem, how do you persevere?\" and \"2. What helps you learn? Are you a visual learner?\"\nTurn 4. TUTOR: Hang on, give me just one second.\nTurn 5. TUTOR: Uh do you see the whiteboard?\nTurn 6. STUDENT: Yes.\nTurn 7. TUTOR: Okay, so we're going to start um our tutoring today with just um two warm-up questions. So, I'm going to have you answer them. So, number one, when you are stuck on a tough problem, how do you persevere personally?\nTurn 8. STUDENT: Um, I normally um like like if I get like stuck on a problem then I um ask my teachers for help for like to help me like what I'm like stuck on.\nTurn 9. TUTOR: Yeah, it's good. It's always good to ask for help.\nTurn 10. TUTOR: Um and then so the second question is what helps you learn? Are you like a visual learner? So like what helps you learn and retain your memory?\nTurn 11. STUDENT: Um what helps me is like um\nTurn 12. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 27 seconds] Tutor erases the text from the whiteboard.\nTurn 12. TUTOR: It's okay if you're stuck. You can you can come back to that.\nTurn 13. TUTOR: All right, so um today we're going to be working on our multiplication and division time tables so we can build repetition and then you can really like memorize so you can like answer the questions quickly, right? That's what we're going to work on today.\nTurn 14. TUTOR: So I have some multiplication tables. Let me find it.\nTurn 15. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 14 seconds]\nTurn 15. TUTOR: Let me get them really quickly.\nTurn 16. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 25 seconds]\nTurn 16. TUTOR: Sorry.\nTurn 17. TUTOR: Here we go.\nTurn 18. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 9 seconds] A new tab opens on the whiteboard showing a multiplication table worksheet. It has a title \"Multiplication Tables\", a space for a name, and a 12x12 grid.\nTurn 18. TUTOR: Can you see um can you see that whiteboard that I opened up?\nTurn 19. STUDENT: Yes.\nTurn 20. TUTOR: The multiplication time tables. So we're just going to review these um really quickly. Can you see everything? I just want to make sure.\nTurn 21. STUDENT: Mhm.\nTurn 22. TUTOR: Okay, so let's start off with our basic multiplication rules. So you know that anything times a number times one is going to equal what? The same number, right?\nTurn 23. STUDENT: Mhm.\nTurn 24. TUTOR: You know that. So one times one, two times one.\nTurn 25. TUTOR: So let's focus on Can you use the text tool?\nTurn 26. TUTOR: Are you able to?\nTurn 27. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 5 seconds]\nTurn 27. TUTOR: Yeah, okay. So let's start off. Um or would you rather do... Let's start off. So what is...\nTurn 28. TUTOR: Oh, sorry.\nTurn 29. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 5 seconds]\nTurn 29. TUTOR: What's two times two?\nTurn 30. STUDENT: Four.\nTurn 31. TUTOR: Four, yeah.\nTurn 32. TUTOR: What's two times three?\nTurn 33. STUDENT: Five.\nTurn 34. TUTOR: Two times three. Two plus three is five.\nTurn 35. STUDENT: Six.\nTurn 36. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 7 seconds] Tutor switches back to the blank whiteboard and writes \"2 x 3\" in red.\nTutor writes \"= 2 + 2 + 2\" in red next to \"2 x 3\".\nTurn 36. TUTOR: Would you like me to do a visual? Oh yeah. Like when we draw on the whiteboard, draw it out? Yeah, okay.\nTurn 37. TUTOR: Let's go on over to let's go back to whiteboard one. Can you go back there?\nTurn 38. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 39. TUTOR: So multiplication is essentially just addition multiple times, right?\nTurn 40. STUDENT: Mhm.\nTurn 41. TUTOR: So when we have the number two times three.\nTurn 42. TUTOR: That's basically just saying two but three times. So this is also just equal to two plus two plus two.\nTurn 43. TUTOR: You know how to can you do that? Two plus two plus two?\nTurn 44. STUDENT: Yeah.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nProvide targeted scaffolding now. Identify the specific unresolved obstacle in the student's current mathematical work. Offer only the smallest useful hint, representation, or clarification that makes the next step accessible. Use the learner's actual numbers and ideas when available. Leave the key calculation or inference for the student and invite one manageable next step. Do not give a full solution, stack several leading questions, repeat an explanation the learner already understands, or invent unseen worksheet details.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
